    "q-118558": {
      "dom_id": "step-q118558",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "The population $y(t)$ of beavers in a river follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=0.2y(8-\\frac{y}{100}),\\,y(0)=200,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible beaver population?",
      "worked_solution": "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible population. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200098/assets/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =0.2y(8-\\frac{y}{100}) \\\\  & =0.2y(\\frac{800-y}{100}) \\\\  & =\\frac{0.2y}{100}(800-y) \\\\  & =0.002y(800-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=800.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $800,$ and we conclude that the number of beavers cannot exceed $800.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
