    "q-119848": {
      "dom_id": "step-q119848",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "The population $y(t)$ of elephants on a certain African wildlife reserve follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=26y-0.0002{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=120,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible population of elephants?",
      "worked_solution": "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible population. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200098/assets/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =26y-0.0002{y}^{2} \\\\  & =0.0002y(130\\,000-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=130\\,000.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $130\\,000,$ and we conclude that the number of elephants cannot exceed $130\\,000.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
