    "q-353486": {
      "dom_id": "step-q353486",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "The population $P(t)$ of elk in a protected national park follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=5P-0.001{P}^{2},\\,P(0)=200,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, measured in years. For which value of $P$ is the elk population growing at the fastest possible rate?\n\n$P=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFor a logistic growth model, the fastest rate of growth occurs when $P=\\frac{1}{2}a.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200098/assets/9019b26b417c757bbfab79afb39379c1fc7a892bc44e934c6f23c339deacfd68.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{dt} & =5P-0.001{P}^{2} \\\\  & =0.001P(5000-P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=5000.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $5000,$ and $P$ grows at its fastest possible rate when \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{1}{2}a=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5000=2500.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
